# Before You Start

Please:

- Read the repository README.
- Review existing Issues.
- Check whether someone is already working on the task.
- Open a Discussion or Issue for significant changes before writing code.

This helps avoid duplicate work.

---

# Contribution Workflow

## Step 1

Fork the repository.

---

## Step 2

Create a feature branch.

Example:

feature/dashboard-improvements

bugfix/sql-query

docs/update-readme

---

## Step 3

Make your changes.

Please keep commits focused and well organised.

---

## Step 4

Test your work.

Where applicable, ensure:

- Code runs successfully
- Documentation is updated
- Existing functionality is not broken

---

## Step 5

Commit using clear commit messages.

Examples:

feat: add customer segmentation model

fix: resolve authentication bug

docs: improve installation guide

refactor: optimise SQL queries

---

## Step 6

Push your branch.

---

## Step 7

Open a Pull Request.

Provide:

- Clear title
- Description
- Motivation
- Screenshots (if applicable)
- Related Issue

---

# Coding Standards

We value readable, maintainable, and well-documented code.

Please:

- Write clean code.
- Keep functions focused.
- Use meaningful variable names.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity.
- Add comments where needed.
- Follow language-specific best practices.

---

# Documentation Standards

Documentation should be:

- Clear
- Accurate
- Beginner-friendly
- Up to date

Whenever functionality changes, update the relevant documentation.

---

# Pull Request Review

Every Pull Request may be reviewed for:

- Code quality
- Performance
- Documentation
- Security
- Maintainability
- Testing
- Alignment with project goals

Maintainers may request changes before approving.

---

# Issue Guidelines

When opening an Issue:

Include:

- Clear title
- Description
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected behaviour
- Actual behaviour
- Environment (if relevant)

Feature requests should explain:

- The problem
- Proposed solution
- Expected impact

---

# Communication

Please communicate respectfully and professionally.

Constructive feedback is encouraged.

Healthy technical discussions are welcome.

Personal attacks are not.

